Here is a simple and clear DevOps explanation of AWS Organizations SCP (Service Control Policies) with real-time examples 👇

✅ What is AWS Organization SCP?
SCP = Service Control Policy
It is a policy at the Organization level that controls what AWS accounts are ALLOWED or DENIED to do.

👉 SCPs DO NOT give permission
👉 They only limit permissions
👉 They apply to all IAM users, roles, and even root user in that account

Think of SCP as:

🔒 Master lock for all AWS accounts under the Organization
🔒 IAM policies work inside the account
🔒 SCP decides what IAM is allowed to allow

🎯 Why are SCPs used (DevOps View)?
SCPs help DevOps enforce:

Security guardrails

Compliance rules

Restrictions in Production

Controls for all teams using AWS

Prevent human mistakes

Avoid disaster (accidental delete or exposure)

Big companies use SCPs to prevent dangerous operations globally.

🛠 Real Time Examples of SCP (Most Common in Companies)
Below are actual SCP examples used by real DevOps teams.

1️⃣ Deny S3 Bucket without Encryption
Prevents any team from creating unencrypted S3 bucket.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "s3:CreateBucket",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotEquals": {
          "s3:x-amz-bucket-object-lock-enabled": "true"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
2️⃣ Deny making S3 bucket public
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "s3:PutBucketPublicAccessBlock",
        "s3:PutBucketAcl",
        "s3:PutBucketPolicy"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
3️⃣ Deny IAM User Creation (Enterprise Best Practice)
Companies want to use SSO, NOT IAM users.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "iam:CreateUser",
        "iam:CreateAccessKey",
        "iam:DeleteUser"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
4️⃣ Deny root account access
Prevents anyone from using the root user except break-glass.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "*",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": "arn:aws:iam::*:root"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
5️⃣ Deny deleting CloudTrail logs
This protects logs and ensures security compliance.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": [
        "cloudtrail:DeleteTrail",
        "s3:DeleteObject"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
6️⃣ Deny EC2 creation without mandatory tags
Enforces tagging policy across all accounts.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "ec2:RunInstances",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "Null": {
          "aws:RequestTag/Owner": "true"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
🎯 Important Points About SCP (Must Know)
1️⃣ SCP only limits, never grants
Even if SCP allows “ec2:*”, IAM also must allow it.

2️⃣ SCP applies even to root user
Root cannot override SCP.
This gives DevOps strong control.

3️⃣ SCP applied at OU or account
If OU has SCP → applies to all accounts inside it.

You can attach SCP to specific accounts.

4️⃣ Used heavily in Prod environments
To avoid accidental mistakes by developers.

5️⃣ SCP affects only Member Accounts
Not the management account.

