✅ SCP vs IAM Policy (High-Level Summary)
Feature	IAM Policy	SCP (Service Control Policy)
Purpose	Grant or deny permissions to users/roles	Set the maximum permissions an AWS account can use
Applies to	Users, Roles, Groups	Entire AWS Accounts, OUs
Can grant permission?	✅ Yes	❌ No
Can only restrict?	❌ No (can allow too)	✅ Yes
Affects root user?	❌ No	✅ Yes
Where applied?	Inside the AWS account	AWS Organizations level
Who uses it?	DevOps, developers, admins	Organization administrators
Scope	Single account only	Multiple accounts
Can override IAM policy?	❌ No	✅ Yes, SCP restricts IAM
Use case	Fine-grained access control	Organization-wide guardrails / security boundaries
🎯 Detailed Comparison
1️⃣ What they control
✔ IAM Policy
Controls what a specific user/role can do
(inside one AWS account)

✔ SCP
Controls what the entire account is allowed or NOT allowed to do, even if IAM allows it.

2️⃣ Who gets affected?
IAM Policy
Only:

IAM users

IAM roles

IAM groups

NOT root user.

SCP
Affects:

All IAM users

All IAM roles

All STS tokens

Root user (YES)

3️⃣ Permission evaluation logic
⭐ IAM Policy = Permission Granted vs Denied
Example:
IAM policy allows:

ec2:StartInstances
Then user can start EC2 (if SCP doesn't block it).

⭐ SCP = Permission Boundary
It sets a hard limit.

Even if IAM policy ALLOWS something…
If SCP DENIES → access is denied.

4️⃣ Understanding with Real-Life Example
🧪 Scenario:
IAM user has:

✔ Allow EC2 Start
✔ Allow EC2 Stop

But the SCP for the account:

❌ Deny EC2 StopInstances

Result:
Start EC2 → Allowed (IAM allows + SCP allows)

Stop EC2 → Denied (IAM allows BUT SCP denies)

👉 SCP wins
👉 SCP is the outer boundary
👉 IAM is inner permissions

